---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { SITE, SUBSTACK } from '../consts';
import portrait from '../assets/felipe-plets-portrait.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${SITE.name}`}
  description="Felipe Plets: engineering leader in Toronto, writing code since 1999 and leading teams since 2006. Now focused on AI-native teams and delivery quality."
>
  <section class="border-b border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-20 pt-40 lg:px-8 lg:pt-48">
      <div class="grid items-end gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[1.4fr_1fr] lg:gap-16">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>About</p>
          <h1 class="mt-5 text-4xl leading-[1.1] sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl" data-reveal>
            Since 1999, circling one question.
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted" data-reveal>
            How do you keep software honest while the pressure to ship keeps rising?
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-line" data-reveal>
          <Image
            src={portrait}
            alt="Felipe Plets"
            widths={[360, 560, 720]}
            sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 24rem, 70vw"
            class="h-auto w-full object-cover contrast-[1.02] saturate-[0.9]"
            loading="eager"
          />
          <div
            class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-ink via-ink/15 to-transparent"
          >
          </div>
          <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 bg-copper/25 mix-blend-soft-light"></div>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20 lg:px-8 lg:py-28">
      <div class="space-y-16">
        <div data-reveal>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Where it started</h2>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <p>
              I started programming in 1999 as a hobby, experimenting with JavaScript
              because it was the fastest way to make something real appear on a screen. It
              did not stay a hobby for long. Over the years I shipped production systems in
              Delphi, PHP, C#, Swift, Kotlin, Python, Ruby, ABAP and Java, and still, a
              great deal of JavaScript.
            </p>
            <p>
              I studied in Brazil, eventually completing a degree at Unisinos, and
              published academic work on ubiquitous computing. That included UbiHealth, a
              collaborative telemedicine system designed to improve diagnosis quality in
              regions of Brazil underserved by doctors. The through-line from that work to
              what I do now is the same: technology is only interesting when it changes an
              outcome someone actually cares about.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div data-reveal>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Leading teams</h2>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <p>
              I have worked in leadership positions since 2006, wearing whichever title the
              organization needed at the time: software developer, team lead, coach,
              consultant. The substance rarely changed. I was designing, implementing,
              coaching and mentoring teams on how to incrementally adopt better practices,
              and measuring whether productivity, quality, performance and security
              actually improved as a result.
            </p>
            <p>
              That work has taken me across several countries and through every team
              arrangement there is: co-located rooms, groups distributed across time zones,
              and fully remote organizations. Each one changes what good communication and
              good review actually look like, and very little of that transfers
              automatically from one setup to the next.
            </p>
            <p>
              Somewhere in there my wife and I immigrated from Brazil to Canada, an
              experience we documented publicly, and one that shaped how I think about
              internationalization in the tech industry and about what it costs people to
              start over. I have volunteered as a career mentor, and I still think giving
              back to the community that raised me professionally is part of the job.
            </p>
            <p>Today I work as a director of engineering.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div data-reveal>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">What I focus on now</h2>
          <div class="mt-5 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <p>
              Code volume is exploding. Vibe coding and agentic engineering have made it
              trivially easy to produce more software than any team can carefully read.
              Meanwhile the cost of poor quality has not moved: rework, maintenance and
              technical debt still consume an enormous share of engineering capacity, and
              risk still arrives through every dependency a developer pulls in.
            </p>
            <p>
              So the interesting work is no longer about generating output. It is about
              designing engineering practices where quality holds as velocity increases:
              guardrails that are real, review that scales, alignment on how the team uses
              models, and decisions grounded in evidence rather than opinion.
            </p>
            <p>
              I write about all of it in
              <a
                href={SUBSTACK.url}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="link-underline text-amber">{SUBSTACK.name}</a
              >, and I work with engineering leaders who are living the same problem.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rule my-16"></div>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4" data-reveal>
        <Button href={SUBSTACK.url} external withArrow>Read the newsletter</Button>
        <Button href="/#contact" variant="ghost">Get in touch</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
